data: {"type":"TOOL_CALL_RESULT","toolCallId":"call_02_ovie87IiZXw0NPmnCNwn2206","messageId":"37b308dd-3f34-48c5-a729-1301a873d836","content":"{\"ok\":true,\"result\":{\"path\":\"/skills/ag-ui-frontend/SKILL.md\",\"content\":\"1: ---\\n2: name: ag-ui-frontend\\n3: description: Use for frontend consumption of AG-UI messages, shared state, run status, executions, interrupts, and controlled Application Events, Frontend Tools, Agent-controlled frontend capabilities, and browser/client tool execution through project Runtime hooks.\\n4: compatibility: One AG-UI Agent Runtime per generated frontend; Phase 8 permits Plugin source writes while Runtime remains read-only.\\n5: allowed-tools: enable_creator_tools read_file ls glob grep edit_file\\n6: ---\\n7: \\n8: # AG-UI Frontend\\n9: \\n10: Maintain this data flow:\\n11: \\n12: ```text\\n13: AG-UI / Mock transport -> Agent Runtime -> Runtime Context Hooks -> UI Plugin -> project UI stack\\n14: ```\\n15: \\n16: ## Current project contract\\n17: \\n18: - `useAgentMessages()` returns normalized Agent messages, including user, assistant, reasoning, activity, and tool-related messages represented by Runtime Core.\\n19: - `useAgentState<TState>()` returns shared Agent state and must be narrowed safely before property access.\\n20: - `useAgentRun().status` is `idle`, `running`, `awaiting-input`, or `error`; a run error carries the user-visible failure detail.\\n21: - `useAgentExecutions()` exposes each Tool, Reasoning, Step, and Subagent lifecycle directly. Never replace local execution status with a generic Run loading flag.\\n22: - `useAgentInterrupts()` returns only current pending interrupts.\\n23: - `usePluginActions()` exposes instance-scoped Agent commands; `useAgentRuntimeActions()` exposes only Agent Runtime commands. Runtime-only UI state belongs in a named service or runtime store, not in AppUIModel composition.\\n24: - `usePluginEvents()` is the inbound, live-only Application Event channel. A Plugin may subscribe only to names declared in its manifest and registered by `/agent-contract/agent-events.ts`.\\n25: - `usePluginService()` remains the component API for named Plugin capabilities.\\n26: - `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts` owns the explicit set of frontend capability operations advertised to the Agent.\\n27: \\n28: ## Boundaries\\n29: \\n30: - Do not instantiate an AG-UI client or manage a second Agent Runtime inside a Plugin.\\n31: - Do not invent a separate public message or state protocol when the Plugin Contract already exposes AG-UI data.\\n32: - Event, snapshot, delta, streaming, tool-call, and error normalization belongs in the frontend Runtime layer; Plugins render the normalized hook values.\\n33: - Never expose or import AG-UI `CUSTOM`, `CustomEvent`, `BaseEvent`, `RawEvent`, `rawEvent`, or `@ag-ui/core` from Plugin code. For a backend-originated, application-specific, transient occurrence with no standard AG-UI semantic, define its payload schema in `/agent-contract/agent-events.ts` before declaring it in `manifest.data.events`. Preserve explicitly supplied event names exactly; recommend lowercase dot-separated naming only when the application has not chosen a name, and never guess an unknown backend payload shape.\\n34: - Frontend Tools use standard AG-UI `RunAgentInput.tools`, `TOOL_CALL_*`, `ToolMessage`, and a continuation Run. Never encode a frontend Tool call as `CUSTOM`, a new UserMessage, or a Plugin-specific protocol.\\n35: - The Agent calls a capability adapter, never React or a Plugin. Reuse or define a stable Service seam, let a Provider Plugin declare and provide that Service, then expose only the product-authorized operations in `/agent-contract/agent-tools.ts`.\\n36: - Tool names use `lower_snake_case`; schemas use `z.strictObject(...)` as the single validation and JSON Schema source; handlers resolve Services at execution time and return short serializable results.\\n37: - Do not infer that every Service method should be exposed. Capability ownership and Agent exposure permission are separate decisions.\\n38: - Plugins must never self-register Frontend Tools or import AG-UI Tool types. They only provide capabilities through the existing Service lifecycle.\\n39: - Do not use Application Events for persistent state, activity progress, standard lifecycle, local Plugin-to-Plugin communication, or frontend-to-backend commands. Application Events have no replay, persistence, or history.\\n40: - A request for different layout or placement is not an AG-UI Runtime change.\\n41: - During Phase 8, implement AG-UI presentation inside `/plugins/` while keeping Runtime and Framework source read-only.\\n42: \\n43: For interaction semantics, reason from product needs and AG-UI first. Treat external Agent UI projects as references, not runtime dependencies or public contracts.\\n44: \\n45: ## History, attachments, speech and feedback services\\n46: \\n47: These capabilities have built-in defaults and project-owned service wiring.\\n48: Business URLs/auth belong in implementation code, not Agent props. Before connecting a business history, upload, transcription or feedback\\n49: service, read [service configuration](references/service-configuration.md).\\n50: Users supply endpoint/authentication/payload details. Creator edits wiring and uses the confirmed private implementation change tools\\n51: for services/ HTTP integration. Explain operation and shared impact before\\n52: confirmation; public Service contract changes use their separate tools. Report\\n53: applied changes and verification accurately. Preserve explicit custom adapters and Plugin layout.\\n54: \\n55: ## Frontend Tool execution and replay contract\\n56: \\n57: For capability or renderer integration details, read the packaged\\n58: [Frontend Tool lifecycle reference](references/frontend-tool-lifecycle.md).\\n59: \\n60: 1. Product side effects belong only in Tool `execute`: open dialog, navigate,\\n61:    select item, change editor file, modify application state, write storage, copy\\n62:    clipboard, download, call mutation API, submit form, or trigger a host action.\\n63: 2. A Tool renderer is a replayable projection. Assume history load, thread/branch\\n64:    switch, remount, React StrictMode and virtualization can mount it again.\\n65:    `render / mount useEffect != tool invocation`.\\n66: 3. Never implement Agent behavior with\\n67:    `useEffect(() => capability.doSomething(), [])`. Instead use\\n68:    `execute -> capability.doSomething()` and `render -> args/result/status`.\\n69:    UI measurement, ResizeObserver, subscription cleanup, focus and animation\\n70:    effects remain legitimate.\\n71: 4. Results must carry a historical receipt sufficient to render args/result/status\\n72:    without checking today's application state. An opened dialog result can contain\\n73:    `{ opened: true, title: \\\"Settings\\\" }`; history does not require it to stay open.\\n74: 5. History restoration must never execute Frontend Tools. An execute on history\\n75:    load is a Runtime bug; do not patch it with renderer `isHistory` workarounds.\\n76: 6. Keep `Plugin -> Service`, `Application agent-tools -> allowed operation`,\\n77:    `Runtime adapter -> Agent UI native frontend execute`. Plugins never\\n78:    register Tools. Preserve root-only execution scope until subagent attribution\\n79:    has its own contract. Do not add another TOOL_CALL executor or CUSTOM protocol.\\n80: \\n81: ## Official integrations\\n82: \\n83: When Agent UI provides a specialized integration:\\n84: \\n85: 1. Inspect the official integration at the project's pinned upstream revision first.\\n86: 2. Reuse its public semantic contracts where possible.\\n87: 3. Do not bypass AgentUICreator application permission.\\n88: 4. Model reusable adaptation as an Integration, not as a Demo Plugin.\\n89: \\n90: React Hook Form: reuse `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/form-tools.formTools` through\\n91: `integration/react-hook-form`. Use ordinary RHF `useForm()` plus an application\\n92: Service and `createReactHookFormFrontendTools({ serviceName, fields, expose })`.\\n93: `expose` is mandatory and has no default. Do not call `useAssistantForm()` or\\n94: `aui.modelContext.register(...)`; combining those with the factory creates duplicate\\n95: registration and bypasses application permission.\\n96: \\n97: Integration installation != automatic Agent permission. Only application\\n98: frontend-tool modules included in the generated allowlist expose operations.\\n99: Integrations can be pluginless and tool-less. Demo consumers own UI, field names,\\n100: Service lifecycle and replayable receipts; the Integration owns reusable contracts.\\n101: \\n102: ## Agent UI Generative UI\\n103: \\n104: `@za38.03/agent-ui-plugins/generative-ui` is the canonical component vocabulary and\\n105: renderer. Prefer official vocabulary before creating custom components. Inspect\\n106: the project's installed version: do not infer capabilities from unreleased\\n107: source or from the presence of related CSS selectors.\\n108: \\n109: - Install optional `integration/generative-ui` for the shared factory, official\\n110:   styled vocabulary, Action Registry and public component export. These files\\n111:   are managed together as one optional Integration, not a default Foundation.\\n112: - `createAgentUIGenerativeUI` exposes `present()` and `promptUser()` capabilities.\\n113:   Capability availability does not grant Agent permission. Do not automatically\\n114:   mount or advertise `present` / `prompt_user`; explicit Tool authorization requires a separate product decision.\\n115: - Use the pinned package types and official Form `$input` collection; do not infer availability from CSS selectors.\\n116: - Keep optional source provenance and official styles under Source Registry;\\n117:   regenerate from the pinned package-owned upstream source. CSS permits only\\n118:   mechanical `.agent-ui-conversation` selector scoping, with no visual redesign.\\n119: \\n120: ## Localization ownership\\n121: \\n122: Vendor-owned code must remain locale-agnostic and unmodified; all product\\n123: localization is owned by AgentUICreator composition, adapters, plugins, and Host\\n124: UI. New user-visible copy must use the existing `useAgentUILocale` namespace and\\n125: complete `en-US` / `zh-CN` dictionaries. Do not create per-Plugin i18n hooks or\\n126: language conditionals. Preserve protocol IDs, enums, schema and persisted fields;\\n127: locale is a view concern. Prefer public props/composition, then owned adapters.\\n128: If upstream lacks a seam, record a localization gap instead of modifying vendor.\\n129: Include `pnpm check:i18n` where available and check new copy during upgrades.\\n130: \",\"hash\":\"377c0469041a453cec1e9c7389fb58927779bacfa14f4c59c69dc22c8bea077f\",\"totalLines\":130,\"nextOffset\":null}}","role":"tool"}

